Example: For an input array a = [10, 2, 5, 10, 10, 1, 2, 5, 10], k = 10, the function should return the indices of the longest sub-array that satisfies the sum constraint.

The provided recursive approach has O(2^n) complexity and is inefficient for large inputs. A more optimal solution uses a sliding window technique with two pointers.

Here's the corrected and optimized implementation:

```java
public static int[] findLongestSubarray(int[] a, int k) {
    if (a == null || a.length == 0) {
        return new int[0];
    }

    int maxLength = 0;
    int startIdx = 0;
    int currentSum = 0;
    int left = 0;

    for (int right = 0; right < a.length; right++) {
        // Add current element to the window
        currentSum += a[right];

        // Shrink window from left while sum exceeds k
        while (currentSum > k && left <= right) {
            currentSum -= a[left];
            left++;
        }

        // Update maximum length if current window is valid
        if (currentSum <= k && (right - left + 1) > maxLength) {
            maxLength = right - left + 1;
            startIdx = left;
        }
    }

    // Return the indices of the longest valid subarray
    return new int[maxLength > 0 ? maxLength : 0];
}
```

